# Level 2 → Level 3

## Concept
This one's about filenames with spaces in them. A space normally tells 
the shell "new argument starts here," so a filename like 
`spaces in this filename` looks like three separate arguments unless 
you tell the shell otherwise.

## Commands used
cat "./--spaces in this filename--"

## Why it works
Wrapping the filename in quotes tells the shell to treat the whole thing 
as one single argument instead of splitting on the spaces. Same job could 
be done by escaping each space individually with a backslash instead.
